# The workbooks are generated, not committed.
#
# They are the format the user actually works in, and every trap the
# office module learned to detect lives in it — merged blocks, a filter
# left on, a total row, a hand-typed money column, more rows than one
# read shows. None of that can exist in a CSV.
#
# But a binary fixture in a repository is a file nobody can review in a
# diff. So the content lives in delfin/agent/benchmark_fixtures.py as a
# spec that reads as a table, and the runner materialises it before the
# snapshot is taken. Rebuild by hand with:
#
#     python -c "from delfin.agent.benchmark_fixtures import \
#         ensure_office_fixtures as f; print(f())"
*.xlsx
.fixture-stamp
